{
  "families": {
    "bone": {
      "label": "Bone",
      "note": "One ground for all five — #FCFCFC, a true neutral white with no tint in it — and one foreground weight. What changes is the chrome value (how far the sidebar and tab strip separate from the page) and how much of the syntax stays warm. Read them left to right as the chrome gets heavier and the hues get cooler.",
      "semantic": {
        "dark": {
          "add": "#7FD962",
          "delete": "#F26D6D",
          "err": "#F26D6D",
          "info": "#59C2FF",
          "mod": "#FFC44D",
          "ok": "#7FD962",
          "warn": "#FFC44D"
        },
        "light": {
          "add": "#4CA750",
          "delete": "#E65050",
          "err": "#E65050",
          "info": "#399EE6",
          "mod": "#E6A700",
          "ok": "#4CA750",
          "warn": "#E6A700"
        }
      }
    },
    "cold": {
      "label": "Cold Front",
      "note": "The one theme here with a colour-relationship inherited from Ayu — a full warm keyword against cool syntax. The original chrome read as ice; both variants replace it with a neutral stone that sits quietly under the page and keeps the selection colourless.",
      "semantic": {
        "dark": {
          "add": "#5CC0A0",
          "delete": "#EC7A7A",
          "err": "#EC7A7A",
          "info": "#62B8EE",
          "mod": "#E0B15A",
          "ok": "#5CC0A0",
          "warn": "#E0B15A"
        },
        "light": {
          "add": "#3F8F6F",
          "delete": "#D24B4B",
          "err": "#D24B4B",
          "info": "#2E86C8",
          "mod": "#C08A1E",
          "ok": "#3F8F6F",
          "warn": "#C08A1E"
        }
      }
    },
    "mono": {
      "label": "Monoline",
      "note": "One accent, one job. Every structural token — tags, functions, keywords, properties, operators, punctuation — is ink at five strengths, and the accent is spent only on literals: strings and numbers, the values you actually hunt for in a file. Tag and function names sit darkest, keywords recede to mid grey, punctuation goes pale. The variants differ only in which single hue gets the literals.",
      "semantic": {
        "dark": {
          "add": "#7FBE8C",
          "delete": "#E2695A",
          "err": "#E2695A",
          "info": "#6E9EE8",
          "mod": "#D2A05A",
          "ok": "#7FBE8C",
          "warn": "#D2A05A"
        },
        "light": {
          "add": "#3C7048",
          "delete": "#C0392B",
          "err": "#C0392B",
          "info": "#2F5FA8",
          "mod": "#A5720F",
          "ok": "#3C7048",
          "warn": "#A5720F"
        }
      }
    },
    "news": {
      "label": "Newsprint",
      "note": "Hue is rationed. Structure is carried by ink at four strengths — black, mid, grey, pale — and exactly one or two colours are let in for keywords and literals, which are the only things you actually scan for. The ground moves between white and stone; nothing on the surface is tinted.",
      "semantic": {
        "dark": {
          "add": "#86C08A",
          "delete": "#E06A78",
          "err": "#E06A78",
          "info": "#8AA4E8",
          "mod": "#D2A85A",
          "ok": "#86C08A",
          "warn": "#D2A85A"
        },
        "light": {
          "add": "#3F6B3F",
